{#
   The panel's own document.

   Not a variant of `layout.html.twig` and not a flag on it: the site's layout
   carries a search bar, a currency switcher, a cookie notice, a footer of 231
   city links, and fourteen external assets -- jQuery, Bootstrap, Font Awesome,
   sweetalert, a range slider, a datepicker. Measured on the sign-in page
   before this existed: 48KB of HTML and eighteen link/script tags, for one
   password field. None of it is for the person running the site (A3.5, #230).

   **Bootstrap came back in A10.1 (#287)**, on purpose and scoped to the panel:
   growing panel functionality changed the tradeoff A3.5 made, and a licensed
   dashboard template (Orchid) gives tables, forms, cards and charts already
   built. What did not come back is the CDN -- Bootstrap, its icon font and
   Inter are vendored into `public/` from the official npm packages, the same
   way the typeface already was, so the panel still fetches nothing from off
   this server. The palette, the type and the shell are Orchid's own, as
   bought -- see `admin.css`'s own docblock for why that is not the site's
   teal any more.

   `Routes::EXCLUDE_HEADER_FOOTER` looks like the lever for this and is not: it
   means "emits its own payload", for JSON. This is a different document, not
   the absence of one -- and `Kernel::wrapped()` lets it through untouched
   because it opens with a doctype.
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-admin>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">

    {# Nobody should ever arrive here from a search engine. #}
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">

    <title>{% block title %}Admin{% endblock %} &middot; {{ config('app.name') }}</title>

    {# The stored theme before the first paint, for the reason the site's own
       header gives: a stylesheet cannot read localStorage and a script at the
       foot runs after the paint, so a dark reader would see the light palette
       flash on every navigation. The same key as the public side, so choosing
       dark out there means dark in here. `data-bs-theme` is set alongside
       `data-theme` so Bootstrap's own component colours never disagree with
       ours -- the same pairing `global.js`'s toggle already keeps in step on
       the public site.

       The collapsed sidebar reads the same way and for the same reason: a
       class Bootstrap's `defer`red bundle applied after paint would still
       show the full-width rail for one frame. `data-rail-collapsed` sits on
       `<html>` rather than `<body>`, because `<body>` does not exist yet
       while this still runs in `<head>`. #}
    <script>
        (function () {
            try {
                var choice = window.localStorage.getItem('tb-theme');

                if (choice === 'light' || choice === 'dark') {
                    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', choice);
                    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-bs-theme', choice);
                } else if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches) {
                    /* admin.css already follows prefers-color-scheme on its own tokens;
                       Bootstrap's tokens only flip under data-bs-theme, so it needs setting here too. */
                    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-bs-theme', 'dark');
                }

                if (window.localStorage.getItem('tb-admin-rail-collapsed') === '1') {
                    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-rail-collapsed', '');
                }

                /* No OS-level preference to fall back to here, unlike the
                   theme above -- density is compact only when asked for. */
                if (window.localStorage.getItem('tb-density') === 'compact') {
                    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-density', 'compact');
                }
            } catch (e) {}
        }());
    </script>

    <link href="{{ asset(config('site.directory.css') ~ '/bootstrap.min.css') }}" rel="stylesheet">
    <link href="{{ asset(config('site.directory.css') ~ '/admin.css') }}" rel="stylesheet">
</head>
<body class="shell{% block shell_class %}{% endblock %}">

{# Read once, called once: `flash()` clears the queued message the moment
   it is asked for, so it is captured here into a variable rather than
   called again anywhere below (G3.1, #304).

   The container itself is not conditional any more: `admin.js`'s
   `ajaxBookingForms()` builds its own toast for a `fetch()` answer, since
   there is nothing queued in the session for `flash()` to hand back on a
   page that never reloaded, and it needs a mount point that exists whether
   or not this render happened to carry a flash (G8.3, #338). #}
{% set flash = flash() %}
<div class="toast-container position-fixed top-0 end-0 p-3" style="z-index: 1100;" data-toast-container>
    {% if flash %}
        <div class="toast align-items-center text-bg-{{ flash.tone.bootstrapClass() }} border-0"
             role="{{ flash.tone.value == 'error' ? 'alert' : 'status' }}"
             aria-live="{{ flash.tone.value == 'error' ? 'assertive' : 'polite' }}"
             aria-atomic="true" data-flash-toast>
            <div class="d-flex">
                <div class="toast-body">{{ flash.message }}</div>
                <button type="button" class="btn-close btn-close-white me-2 m-auto" data-bs-dismiss="toast" aria-label="Close"></button>
            </div>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
</div>

{# One shared modal for every `data-confirm` button, rather than one per
   site -- Orchid's own `modalStatic` shape (`ui-modals.html`), including
   its `data-bs-backdrop="static"`: a decision worth confirming is worth
   not losing to a stray Escape or an off-target click. `admin.js`'s
   `confirmFirst()` fills the body and wires the accept button (G3.2,
   #305). #}
<div class="modal fade" id="confirmModal" tabindex="-1" aria-labelledby="confirmModalTitle" aria-hidden="true" data-bs-backdrop="static" data-bs-keyboard="false">
    <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-centered">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="confirmModalTitle">Please confirm</h5>
                <button type="button" class="btn-close" data-bs-dismiss="modal" aria-label="Close"></button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">
                <p class="mb-0" data-confirm-modal-body></p>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-footer">
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-secondary" data-bs-dismiss="modal">Cancel</button>
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" data-confirm-modal-accept>Confirm</button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

{# The command palette -- one shared modal, the same reason `#confirmModal`
   above is one. No header/footer chrome: the search input *is* the header,
   Orchid's own command-palette shape from nowhere in this template set
   (there is no demo page for one), so this is built from the same modal
   primitive rather than a bespoke overlay -- `commandPalette()` in
   `admin.js` fills `[data-palette-results]` and wires the keyboard
   (G4.1, #312). #}
<div class="modal fade" id="commandPalette" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true" aria-labelledby="commandPaletteLabel">
    <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-centered">
        <div class="modal-content palette">
            <h2 class="visually-hidden" id="commandPaletteLabel">Search</h2>
            <div class="palette__search">
                <i class="bi bi-search" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                <input
                    type="text"
                    class="palette__input"
                    data-palette-input
                    placeholder="Search bookings, subscribers, help content…"
                    aria-label="Search bookings, subscribers, help content"
                    autocomplete="off"
                    role="combobox"
                    aria-expanded="true"
                    aria-controls="paletteResults"
                    aria-autocomplete="list"
                >
            </div>
            <div class="palette__results" id="paletteResults" data-palette-results role="listbox" aria-label="Search results"></div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

{% block shell %}
    {# One rail, one well, the rail fixed and permanent beside it -- Orchid's
       own layout, not a flag on the public one. #}
    {% include 'admin/partials/rail.html.twig' %}

    <div class="well">
        {# Chrome only -- a breadcrumb, search, two icon toggles and the
           user menu. Everything about the page itself (title, description,
           its own actions) lives below in the page header instead, which
           is Orchid's own split (`cms-pages.html`'s `.orchid-header`
           versus its page header), not the one row this used to cram both
           into. Search is G4.1 (#312), the user menu G6.4 (#348);
           notifications are still later work (G6.1, #329). #}
        <header class="well__top">
            <div class="well__lead">
                {# One button, two jobs: `railToggle()` in `admin.js` opens the
                   mobile drawer below `lg` and collapses the rail to icons at
                   `lg` and up, the same button Orchid's `data-orchid-sidebar-toggle`
                   is. #}
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-icon well__toggle" data-rail-toggle aria-controls="adminRail" aria-label="Toggle navigation">
                    <i class="bi bi-list" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                </button>

                {# Hidden below `md`, the same breakpoint Orchid's own demo
                   hides it at -- there is not room for both this and the
                   rail toggle on a phone-width screen, and the rail toggle
                   wins. #}
                <nav aria-label="breadcrumb" class="well__breadcrumb d-none d-md-block">
                    <ol class="breadcrumb mb-0">
                        <li class="breadcrumb-item"><a href="/admin" aria-label="Overview"><i class="bi bi-house-door" aria-hidden="true"></i></a></li>
                        {% block breadcrumb %}
                            <li class="breadcrumb-item active" aria-current="page">{{ block('title') }}</li>
                        {% endblock %}
                    </ol>
                </nav>
            </div>

            <div class="well__top-tools">
                {# The command palette's own trigger -- `Ctrl`/`Cmd`+`K` from
                   anywhere opens the same thing, this button is the one for
                   a reader who does not know that yet. Finding a booking
                   used to mean going to Bookings first and typing there;
                   this is what "later work" above turned into (G4.1, #312). #}
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-icon js-search-trigger" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#commandPalette" aria-label="Search (Ctrl+K)">
                    <i class="bi bi-search" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                </button>

                {# One preference for every `.orchid-table` on the panel, not a
                   per-page choice -- `densityToggle()` in `admin.js` (G3.6,
                   #309). Not part of Orchid's own template, but the same kind
                   of tiny global toggle as the theme switch beside it, so it
                   stays in the topbar rather than moving to a page's own
                   actions. #}
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-icon js-density" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Toggle table density">
                    <i class="bi bi-arrows-expand well__density-icon well__density-icon--comfortable" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <i class="bi bi-arrows-collapse well__density-icon well__density-icon--compact" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                </button>

                <button type="button" class="btn btn-icon js-theme well__theme" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Toggle dark theme">
                    <i class="bi bi-sun-fill well__theme-icon well__theme-icon--sun" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <i class="bi bi-moon-stars-fill well__theme-icon well__theme-icon--moon" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                </button>

                {# The other half of "a user menu" (G6.1, #329). Settings and
                   Billing/Help Center still wait -- Settings already has its
                   own rail entry, and there is no billing or help desk here
                   to link to. Profile is G9 (#350): there is no accounts
                   table to read a name/role/email from -- "one password, one
                   session flag, no accounts table" (`AdminController`, A3.2,
                   #100) -- so all three, and the avatar, are
                   `config('meta.author')`, the same block the public
                   footer's own credit line already reads (G6.4, #348), now
                   editable from the Profile page itself. #}
                <div class="dropdown">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-icon well__user" data-bs-toggle="dropdown" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Account menu">
                        <img class="well__user-avatar" src="{{ config('meta.author.avatar') }}" width="32" height="32" alt="">
                        <span class="well__user-meta d-none d-lg-flex">
                            <span class="well__user-name">{{ config('meta.author.name') }}</span>
                            <span class="well__user-role">{{ config('meta.author.role') }}</span>
                        </span>
                        <i class="bi bi-chevron-down d-none d-lg-inline" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    </button>
                    <ul class="dropdown-menu dropdown-menu-end well__user-menu">
                        <li class="well__user-menu-header">
                            <img class="well__user-avatar" src="{{ config('meta.author.avatar') }}" width="40" height="40" alt="">
                            <div class="min-w-0">
                                <p class="mb-0 fw-semibold">{{ config('meta.author.name') }}</p>
                                <small class="text-body-secondary">{{ config('meta.author.email') }}</small>
                            </div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <a class="dropdown-item d-flex align-items-center gap-2" href="/admin/profile">
                                <i class="bi bi-person" aria-hidden="true"></i> Profile
                            </a>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <form method="post" action="/admin/logout">
                                <input type="hidden" name="{{ csrf_field() }}" value="{{ csrf_token() }}">
                                <button type="submit" class="dropdown-item d-flex align-items-center gap-2 text-danger">
                                    <i class="bi bi-box-arrow-right" aria-hidden="true"></i> Sign out
                                </button>
                            </form>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </header>

        {# `tabindex="-1"` so the skip link can put focus here, the same reason
           the public layout gives for its own landing point. #}
        <main class="well__body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
            {# Not sticky -- unlike the topbar above, this scrolls away with
               the content beneath it, the same as Orchid's own page header
               does inside `.orchid-main`. Suppressed whole rather than
               partly: a page with its own hero (Bookings' analytics header,
               G7.1 #332) replaces the title *and* the actions next to it,
               the same as Orchid's own `analytics.html` has no second,
               plainer title above its hero either. #}
            {# `is not defined or page_header`, not `page_header|default(true)`:
               Twig's `default` filter treats `false` itself as "empty" and
               silently overrides it back to the default, which would make a
               page's own `{% set page_header = false %}` do nothing. #}
            {% if page_header is not defined or page_header %}
                <div class="well__page-header">
                    <div>
                        <h1 class="h3 well__title">{% block heading %}{{ block('title') }}{% endblock %}</h1>
                        {% block description %}{% endblock %}
                    </div>

                    <div class="well__tools">
                        {% block tools %}{% endblock %}
                    </div>
                </div>
            {% endif %}

            {% block content %}{% endblock %}
        </main>

        {# Orchid's own shape (`cms-pages.html`'s `.orchid-footer`), this
           app's own content: no fabricated tagline, no dead links. Privacy
           and Terms are real pages this site already has; Orchid's generic
           "Support" becomes Help, the real hub in its place (G6.2, #330).

           What the page cost to build rides on the same line as the
           copyright rather than a column of its own -- the public
           footer's own restraint (`footer/bottom.html.twig`) for the same
           reason: worth keeping on a project whose point is how it is
           built, not worth making the loudest thing in the footer. Free
           to print here: see `LayoutData::footerPerf()` for why this is
           not `stats()` (G6.3, #346). #}
        {% set perf = footer_perf() %}
        <footer class="well__footer" role="contentinfo">
            <p class="mb-0 small text-body-secondary">
                &copy; {{ "now"|date("Y") }} {{ config('app.name') }}.
                {{ perf.execution_time }}s &middot; {{ perf.database_requests }} quer{{ perf.database_requests == 1 ? 'y' : 'ies' }}
            </p>
            <ul class="list-inline mb-0 small">
                <li class="list-inline-item"><a class="text-body-secondary" href="/privacy">Privacy</a></li>
                <li class="list-inline-item"><a class="text-body-secondary" href="/terms">Terms</a></li>
                <li class="list-inline-item"><a class="text-body-secondary" href="/help">Help</a></li>
            </ul>
        </footer>
    </div>
{% endblock %}

<script src="{{ asset(config('site.directory.js') ~ '/bootstrap.bundle.min.js') }}" defer></script>
{# A page's own script, for the one page that needs one -- Chart.js is
   200KB, and every other page in the panel cannot use it (G2.2, #288). Empty
   here, so `AdminShellTest`'s count of this file's own tags is unaffected;
   a page that fills it carries its own tag, in its own template. Before
   `admin.js`, not after: `admin.js`'s own `charts()` reads the `Chart`
   global at parse time, and every script here runs `defer` -- in document
   order, not load order, but order still matters between two deferred
   scripts that depend on each other. #}
{% block scripts %}{% endblock %}
<script src="{{ asset(config('site.directory.js') ~ '/admin.js') }}" defer></script>
</body>
</html>
